#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096417 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096617 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d7a77a89-0535-54da-a246-36ee5b4e9e7f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3634" :question/problem "Differentiate $x\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\cos x .$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t (x\\cos x)' &=   u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t&= (x)'\\cdot \\cos x + x\\cdot (\\cos x)'\\\n\t\t\t\t\t   & = 1 \\cdot \\cos x + x (-\\sin x)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t  & =  \\cos x - x \\sin x\t\t\t\t\t\n\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096618 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6bb0eded-7277-5ab5-8c13-bfc480403b1a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3635" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=x^2\\ln x,$ find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x) = x^2$ and $v(x)=\\ln x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({x}^{2})}^{′}ln⁡x+{x}^{2}{(ln⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot ln⁡x+{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =2xln⁡x+x \\\\  & =x(2ln⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096419 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096619 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "072ce609-b04d-57b0-9c7d-cbb8765c38fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3636" :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal C$ is given by $y=e^x\\sin x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\dfrac \\pi 2.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^x$ and $v(x)=\\sin{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = e^x,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot cos⁡x+sin⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi /2} & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(cos⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+sin⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e^{\\pi/2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096617 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fbb081d-8fbf-5577-b042-226064e10be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571014} {:answer/id #uuid "a20a9a53-3e6f-5754-8799-d24e521ac2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571145} {:answer/id #uuid "a462a7c9-b56d-5b3e-b5f2-01b27202da04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571149} {:answer/id #uuid "cadbcaa0-5f4b-5598-a6eb-4541c20a3865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571202} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2fd5e5-1d32-56a9-b43f-1421f5392621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a462a7c9-b56d-5b3e-b5f2-01b27202da04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571149} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d091bc-16c4-5784-a6c4-28309a12be4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8004d91-2319-5283-80d1-a0670d83fdc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104300" :question/problem "Differentiate ${x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={x}^{3}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({x}^{3})^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+({x}^{3})(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096617 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b194bf2f-feaa-5656-a3ee-0ff466a39e77" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing a Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "329a0351-2458-5dc2-afb3-fb83d076e70a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}sec⁡x+{e}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285251} {:answer/id #uuid "3d41ba8e-fd4a-5c5b-8e2c-3eb30e482c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡x+{e}^{x}tan⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285366} {:answer/id #uuid "5b2ff268-76d1-583f-9abe-62b917b9c7eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}{csc}^{2}⁡x+{e}^{x}tan⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285621} {:answer/id #uuid "93783351-25ff-5c87-8ae9-958c98c327e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x+tan⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286129} {:answer/id #uuid "eb7d1570-7a65-5731-b765-bf52675c003d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x+{e}^{x}tan⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb7d1570-7a65-5731-b765-bf52675c003d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x+{e}^{x}tan⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286945} :answer-field/id #uuid "a44b9e2e-ea9e-5094-bc4f-bc781e7df5d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a541e9ea-7fd8-5568-9e5c-f9ef03f5eaf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-1931" :question/problem "Differentiate ${e}^{x}tan⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of the two functions $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=tan⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({e}^{x}tan⁡x)^{′} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})^{′}\\cdot tan⁡x+{e}^{x}\\cdot (tan⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(tan⁡x)+({e}^{x})({sec}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}{sec}^{2}⁡x+{e}^{x}tan⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096619 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa1cb47c-2e18-5935-a199-4342ba479343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa1cb47c-2e18-5935-a199-4342ba479343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571271} :answer-field/id #uuid "99aa048f-795f-5835-b3e4-7ab1bb44a8a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0fee3ca-7f95-5f0e-a414-4282e409bf7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-319824" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=2x+1$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=1$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)+1}{1}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571385 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096618 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7864c3b-b764-56cd-a641-bd65ea7dd484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec^{2}(x)\\log(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7864c3b-b764-56cd-a641-bd65ea7dd484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec^{2}(x)\\log(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571265} :answer-field/id #uuid "0af8eb9f-fc24-5d73-ad75-f548e3ea3646" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "303e2b76-22b6-55f6-abfa-1986d9ab5f9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-320820" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\operatorname{log}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n**Hint:** $\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)={log}_{10}⁡(x)$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $+\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))}^{′}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x){(\\operatorname{log}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =({sec}^{2}⁡(x))(\\operatorname{log}⁡(x))+(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\operatorname{log}⁡(x)+\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096618 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f15a60dd-f6a7-577d-bdbc-148032de94c8" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product Containing an Exponential or Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddbe9744-04e5-5a13-898b-3293efe4160f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}+3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddbe9744-04e5-5a13-898b-3293efe4160f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}+3}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571233} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6a34341-fe46-5049-b63f-03fb79ef67af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f475b19b-1e35-517e-8f15-4fa3d740b6ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-320824" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=({x}^{2}+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,+$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={x}^{2}+3$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x).$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({x}^{2}+3)}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+({x}^{2}+3){(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))}^{′} \\\\  & =(2x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))+({x}^{2}+3)(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}+3}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096619 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122dcace-6bca-59bb-b96f-a96026e9a833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327088} {:answer/id #uuid "29771cef-6628-575d-b6d2-039fa89b2a35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327286} {:answer/id #uuid "3101f4e9-df22-5461-be7e-f6674a765dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327368} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e4fd4c-0122-574c-8317-faf21984d650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328648} {:answer/id #uuid "e154d586-179b-57cc-acf3-bc95bc04c67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3e4fd4c-0122-574c-8317-faf21984d650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328648} :answer-field/id #uuid "745d0171-7216-5576-96bb-4a33b2c2f9b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1066779d-ebe1-53a9-ad1f-5e9c5ee1f7e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-48517" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=({x}^{3}-1){e}^{x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{3}-1$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =({x}^{3}-1)\\cdot {e}^{x}+{e}^{x}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=2$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={{e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1)|}_{x=2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}({2}^{3}+3(2)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =19{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $19{e}^{2}.$"}]}
